>Here's _my_ way to make NetHack harder:
>
>* Two words: stronger monsters. Currently the only creatures capable of
>busting a character at midgame time are jabberwocks and minotaurs. Tut,
>tut. If the "Hell-only" monsters' attacks contained several d10's or
>d20's, we'd solve half of the problem right there.
>

  Which would completely break the current balancing method of
being mobbed.  Any open space on a level could easily be a
deathtrap: Juiblex, Orcus, the first level of Vlad's, Moloch's
Sanctum, the VotD, etc.  

  Right now getting mobbed five deep can kill a character in the
dungeons who doesn't have a way to immediately escape the level.

  On the Astral Planes, there are no items that let you 
immediately escape the level.  The "zap wand of teleport then
#jump" method would be required.  That doesn't work on the
Planes of Air or Water.



>* Make it so that intrinsic resistances are less powerful than
>extrinsic resistances. Like, they only halve the damage instead of
>negating it entirely. I actually have a patch that does this, but I
>haven't released it yet.

  It's a truism in Nethack that any risk greater than 0.00% is 
too risky over the course of the game.  A quick test shows that
a 99% certainty done 30 times becomes only a 75% probability 
overall.

  And the obvious player response would be more unpopular still:
get more hit points and better AC.  Otherwise getting surrounded
by poisonous snakes when #dipping at a fountain would be just
another form of instadeath.


  (It may also be helpful to note that even 1000 hit points can 
be viewed as just another resistance gained, as 1000 hit points
isn't an instawin, anymore than getting all resistances is an
instawin.)

>
